Keep this preview disconnected from real order lookup. Remove it from production or protect it as an internal preview. Replace the application origin and Papertrail branding before sending, and make sure your actual order page exists.


## Where should the recipient’s language come from?


Use the language saved with the checkout or the customer’s explicit email preference. Resolve it on the server when preparing the confirmation. A later background worker has no useful browser language to consult, and an email-open event is too late to choose its contents.


The example groups German regional tags under German copy and


` de-DE


` amount formatting. Everything else gets English copy and


` en-IE


` formatting. That is a deliberately small policy, not support for every regional convention. Add a locale-to-catalog mapping if Austrian or Swiss formatting needs to differ.


[MDN documents locale canonicalization](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Intl/getCanonicalLocales) and


[locale-aware number formatting](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Intl/NumberFormat) . Translating “Total” does not change punctuation or the currency symbol’s position. Formatting an amount does not convert currencies either.


Keep an order’s language consistent across its subject, preheader, HTML, plain text and later notifications. Persist the resolved language with the notification record. Otherwise a changed account preference could make a retry produce a different message from the first attempt.


## How do missing translations behave?


There are two different failures. An unsupported recipient language selects the complete English catalog. A missing product name in the selected language stops preparation. The latter avoids quietly mixing English product labels into a German confirmation.


TypeScript helps maintain the two in-code catalogs, but it cannot prove a translator supplied good copy or that external data matches


` Order


` . Validate externally loaded dictionaries and order data at your application boundary. Do not cast arbitrary request JSON to


` Order


` and treat that as validation.


Keep product names as text children, not strings assembled into a Paragraph’s


` html


` prop. The URL comes from trusted application configuration and a bounded order reference. Its destination must authenticate the visitor and authorize access to that order; knowing a reference is not permission to see it.


## How do you hand the localized message to Resend?


Put sending in server-only application code, separate from the GET preview. Resend requires an


[API key and verified sending domain](https://resend.com/docs/send-with-nodejs) . Set


` RESEND_API_KEY


` and


` MAIL_FROM


` in your server secret configuration. This helper is for one controlled submission after your application has selected an authorized recipient and eligible order:


```text
import     {     Resend     }     from     'resend'  ;
import     {   orderConfirmation  ,   type   Order     }     from     './order-confirmation'  ;


export     async     function     sendConfirmation  (
order  :     Order  ,     language  :   string  ,     recipient  :   string  ,     origin  :   string  ,
)     {
const   apiKey   =   process  .  env  .  RESEND_API_KEY  ;
const     from     =   process  .  env  .  MAIL_FROM  ;
if     (  !  apiKey   ||     !  from  )     throw     new     Error  (  'Configure server mail credentials'  )  ;
const     {   subject  ,   html  ,   text   }     =     orderConfirmation  (  order  ,   language  ,   origin  )  ;
const     {   data  ,   error   }     =     await     new     Resend  (  apiKey  )  .  emails  .  send  (  {
from  ,     to  :     [  recipient  ]  ,   subject  ,   html  ,   text  ,
}  )  ;
if     (  error   ||     !  data  ?.  id  )     throw     new     Error  (  'Email submission unresolved'  )  ;
return   data  .  id  ;
}
```


The


[send-email API](https://resend.com/docs/api-reference/emails/send-email) accepts HTML and plain text. A provider response is not proof of delivery. This helper has no durable send record or duplicate protection: do not attach it directly to a retrying checkout webhook. Before automating, record an order-specific notification key, freeze its language and payload, and design bounded retries and reconciliation for ambiguous responses.


Use an address you control for the first submission. Never expose this helper as an unauthenticated endpoint accepting arbitrary recipients. Your application owns event eligibility, recipient selection and delivery state; Elements owns rendering. Our


[transactional-email guide](https://unlayer.com/blog/transactional-emails) explains that broader message category.


## What should you review before adding more languages?


Start with long headings and product names at narrow widths, then compare a single-item order with a denser one. Avoid fixed-height text containers. A German label should wrap naturally instead of forcing you to shrink every language’s typography.


Have a fluent reviewer check tone and terminology, including subject lines and reply instructions. The footer assumes a monitored reply address; change it if that is not how your shop provides support. Browser previews help review layout, but you still need the email-client checks relevant to your customers. Neither this template nor a screenshot establishes universal inbox compatibility.


## Frequently asked questions


### Does Elements translate the email automatically?


No. This implementation selects application-owned translations before rendering. Elements turns the resulting React tree into content. Changing the visual editor’s interface language is a different feature.


### Do I need an i18n library for two languages?


Not for this small catalog. A typed dictionary keeps the first version understandable. Introduce a translation library when pluralization, message extraction or translator workflows justify it, and resolve messages before passing them into the layout.


### Can customers edit these translations visually?


Not in this code-first example. A customer-editable experience needs a separate visual-builder workflow and a clear policy for how saved designs relate to translation catalogs. Visual edits do not reconstruct React source. Our


[React builder guide](https://unlayer.com/blog/embeddable-react-email-builders-guide) covers that integration route.


